{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.ConstantOffOnePoint","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Archimedean.ConstantOffOnePoint","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Calculus.MeanValue"],"declarations":1,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.constant_of_hasDerivWithinAt_zero_off_point","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.constant_of_hasDerivWithinAt_zero_off_point","kind":"theorem","line":36,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/ConstantOffOnePoint.lean#L36-L90","doc":"**Continuous, and zero derivative off a single point, implies constant.**\n\n`f` is continuous on `[a,b]` and has vanishing right derivative at every point of\n`[a,b)` *except possibly one*. Then `f` is constant.\n\nThe excepted point costs nothing: on `[a,c]` the hypothesis already covers\n`[a,c)`, and to the right of `c` one applies the standard result on `[t,b]` for\neach `t > c` and passes to the limit. ","signatureChunk":0}]}